1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,150
I am Bangie anak Embol,

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,500
from Sungai Kain,

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:16,800
and I inherited the weaving

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,153
tradition from my grandmothers.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
By the age of 10,

6
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:30,990
I had already mastered weaving

7
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:33,470
and motif tying (ngebat).

8
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:37,950
When I first learned pattern tying (ngebat),

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,800
I made a small Pua.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,920
For my second and third attempts,

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,920
 I created larger pieces.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,920
I learned weaving from my grandmother.

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,270
My mother had passed away, and I was also an only child.

14
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:59,870
After my mother passed away,

15
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:02,870
I lived with my grandmother,

16
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:06,350
who taught me how to weave.

17
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:12,400
I started weaving at the age of 10 and have continued doing so ever since.

18
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:18,420
Besides weaving pua kumbu, I am also skilled in various traditional crafts,

19
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,960
including mat and sun hat (tanggi) weaving,

20
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:29,300
supplementary weaving (nyungkit),

21
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:32,780
additional thread patterning (milih),

22
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:35,270
and embroidery (nyulam).

23
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:40,270
I continue practising these traditional skills

24
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:46,490
to preserve Iban heritage for future generations.

25
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:56,920
After learning the motif-binding technique,

26
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,170
I also learned other techniques,

27
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:00,420
 including menyungkit (pattern lifting), weaving,

28
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:04,570
joining fabric, embroidery and arranging threads.

29
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:06,970
I am able to perform all of these techniques.

30
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:09,620
I share my knowledge and teach others

31
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:12,050
because I never had the opportunity to attend school

32
00:02:12,130 --> 00:02:13,130
and had lost both my parents.

33
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:17,670
That is how I learned from a young age

34
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:20,070
and became skilled in making various

35
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:23,150
traditional Iban crafts.

36
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,920
Initially, my inspiration for

37
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,870
creating pua kumbu came from

38
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:35,410
dreams that stayed in my mind.

39
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:39,950
I do not copy other people's work.

40
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:42,670
For example, the Misna Raja Pun Kayu Kaya motif

41
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:44,200
was inspired by one of my own dreams.

42
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:45,280
After that,

43
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:48,970
I created the Buah Karap Merayong Nanga Panggau motif,

44
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:51,870
which was also inspired by one of my dreams.

45
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:56,050
As the name was very long, I decided to keep it as it was.

46
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:03,660
When I first became a leader among pua kumbu weavers

47
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:08,050
through Saberkas, I gathered the women in our longhouse

48
00:03:08,130 --> 00:03:13,670
to practise ngebat (pattern-tying).

49
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:18,320
I explained that it was not merely a traditional practice

50
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,320
but could also provide a source of income.

51
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:26,470
So all the women in our longhouse joined in to tie patterns.

52
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:31,090
The practice then spread throughout Sungai Kain,

53
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:33,320
Kapit, with more women taking part.

54
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:35,000
My intention was to preserve this traditional heritage

55
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,630
and prevent it from disappearing.

56
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:41,170
In the past,

57
00:03:41,250 --> 00:03:48,050
many buyers came directly to our longhouse

58
00:03:48,130 --> 00:03:49,980
to purchase Pua from us.

59
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:52,820
My desire to continue this effort

60
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:53,950
remains unchanged to this day.

61
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:58,000
I later continued with the ngar process,

62
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:01,870
despite it being difficult and exhausting.

63
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:04,900
This process is essential for the natural red engkudu dye

64
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:08,080
to penetrate the threads properly.

65
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,720
Buyers also prefer Pua dyed with natural colourants

66
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:12,450
rather than synthetic dyes (Pua Selup).

67
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:17,770
The yellow dye was my own discovery during the farming season.

68
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:21,170
I found a yellow-coloured root

69
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:24,170
known as akar penawar landak (porcupine cure).

70
00:04:24,250 --> 00:04:26,900
According to the elders, porcupines eat this root

71
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:30,970
after consuming poisonous plants or fruits.

72
00:04:31,050 --> 00:04:35,630
This is how it got its name.

73
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:40,970
To this day, we still use natural dyes:

74
00:04:41,050 --> 00:04:47,030
akar penawar landak for yellow,

75
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:54,520
renggat or tarum for dark blue or black (celum),

76
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,520
and engkudu for red.

77
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:01,620
My woven Pua can also be washed or soaked

78
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:05,100
for 24 hours without the colours fading.

79
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:10,870
If I say that a Pua is dyed with engkudu, then engkudu is what I use.

80
00:05:10,950 --> 00:05:16,236
However, mixing it with other materials will produce a different result.

81
00:05:16,316 --> 00:05:22,750
I cannot speak on behalf of others, as I do not want to give inaccurate information.

82
00:05:25,950 --> 00:05:30,570
I was first appointed as an Adiguru Kraf (Mastercraftsmen)

83
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:36,470
before being recognised as a Tokoh Kraf Negara (National Mastercraftsmen).

84
00:05:36,550 --> 00:05:41,970
I am truly grateful

85
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:44,970
- to everyone from Kraftangan Malaysia,

86
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:47,970
for this recognition.

87
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:52,820
It has motivated me

88
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:57,220
to continue teaching weaving

89
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:03,750
to both women and men,

90
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:06,970
there is nothing wrong with men learning

91
00:06:07,050 --> 00:06:09,720
if they wish to learn.

92
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:12,070
That is what brings joy to my heart.

93
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:16,650
I am delighted to be invited here by all of you.

94
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:20,620
My heart feels at peace and filled with happiness,

95
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:22,040
even in my old age.

96
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:26,770
From the age of 40 until now,

97
00:06:26,850 --> 00:06:31,030
in my eighties.

98
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:37,470
So these are my words of advice.

99
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:39,890
As I grow older,

100
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:46,770
my hope is that future generations, including my children,

101
00:06:46,850 --> 00:06:52,420
 grandchildren and descendants, will carry on this legacy

102
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:57,200
and continue to lead and guide our next generation.

103
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:02,650
I hope those interested in traditional crafts

104
00:07:02,730 --> 00:07:04,580
will continue my role in passing down

105
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:08,520
the skills of weaving and pattern-tying (ngebat).

106
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:11,520
However, the ngar practice must not be performed

107
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:14,520
without guidance through dreams,

108
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:17,520
we cannot easily perform ngar, as this sacred tradition

109
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,520
must be respected and never treated lightly.

110
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:23,520
That is my heartfelt wish:

111
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:26,520
for future generations to preserve

112
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:32,120
our women's traditional crafts

113
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:38,170
and prevent them from disappearing.

114
00:07:38,250 --> 00:07:43,270
I hope this heritage will continue

115
00:07:43,350 --> 00:07:46,350
to improve household income.

